  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThe models had done such a good job the last few days bringing\nday after day of moderate to heavy rain to the region. Today's\nshort range models had the thunderstorms moving into El Paso,\nHudspeth and Otero Counties this evening, but it looks like some\ndrier and more stable air has pushed further east which is keeping\nmost of the thunderstorm activity confined to Hudspeth County.\nThat drying trend will continue on Saturday and Sunday, as the\ntrough aloft that had helped pull moisture into the region, pushes\nthrough the inter mountain west into the upper midwest. On the\nback side of the system drier air will move into the area, \ngreatly reducing or even eliminating our rain chances on Saturday \nand Sunday. Precipitable water values (PW's) today were in the \nrange of 1.1 to 1.3, but by Sunday we will see PW values in the \nrange of 0.5 to 0.8. Anytime you are below an inch on PW's it is \nmuch harder to get thunderstorms going. \n\nFor Monday we will begin to see a pattern change back to wetter\nweather. An upper level ridge will begin to strengthen across\neastern Texas while at the same time an upper level trough will \napproach the area through southern California. The circulation \naround these systems will begin to draw up moisture. Initially on \nMonday the moisture will not be very deep, so Monday is looking \npretty dry. But by Tuesday the PW values will jump up to 1.3 to \n1.5 which is well above average for this time of year. Those \nhigher PW values continue through the end of next week. Short wave\ntroughs embedded in the southerly flow will help kick off daily \nthunderstorms and with the higher PW values, the thunderstorms \nnext week will be very efficient rain produces. The ECMWF even brings\nthe remnants of a tropical system into western New Mexico on next\nThursday but the latest run of the GFS doesn't have any such \nsystem. Whether there is a remnant tropical system or simply a \ndeep fetch of tropical moisture, next week looks to have a good \npotential for heavy rain and flash flooding. Plan on checking the \nlatest forecast during this weekend if you have outdoor plans next\nweek.\n\nLooking at temperatures, with the drier air moving into the \nregion we will see less clouds and more sun. This will help keep \nour afternoon high temperatures a few degrees above average, but \nthe drier air will allow for cooler and pleasant morning low \ntemperatures. Then as the moisture, clouds and rain chances return\nto the region we will see high temperatures dip to 4 to 7 degrees\nbelow average for the middle and end of next week. Who's ready \nfor some below average high temperatures?",
